# Two comparable Ba-MOFs with similar linkers for enhanced CO2 capture and separation by introducing N-rich groups

Two comparable Ba-MOFs with similar linkers for enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation by introducing N-rich groups
Abstract Two new different metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) $\mathrm{\Delta[Ba(L1)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ (MOF 1) and $\scriptstyle{[\mathrm{Ba}(\mathrm{L}2)(-}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{l}_{n}{\cdot}0.5n\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}0.5n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (MOF 2) were yielded by the assembly of oxygen-friendly $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ba}}(\mathrm{II})$ ions and two similar linkers, namely 2-(imidazol-1-yl)terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{L}1\dot{\iota}$ ) and 2-(1H-1,2,4-triazol-1-yl) terephthalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}2)$ . Single-crystal X-ray diffractions (XRD) indicate that MOF 1 is a new three-dimensional (3D) stacking dense network formed by the one-dimensional (1D) rod-shaped chains and L1 linkers, whereas MOF 2 presents a 3D nanotube porous framework with cylindrical tunnels based on the 1D loop chains as the secondary building units (SBUs) by replacing the imidazole group in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}1$ with the triazole group in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}2$ . As a result, MOF 2 has a higher density of active sites and Lewis acid sites in the porous surface of nanotube than MOF 1. Thereby, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation capacity of MOF 2 is great higher than that of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ .
Keywords MOFs; Similar linkers; Secondary building units (SBUs); $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture; Isosteric heat
## 1 Introduction
The adsorbent materials have drawn much more attention due to their wide applications in ion exchange, catalysis gas capture and separation, which often have large specific surface area and suitable pore size, such as zeolites, activated carbon as well as molecular sieve [1–4]. Although many kinds of microporous materials have been explored for diverse applications, they still have some limitations. For instance, there are key problems in terms of selectivity and cycling stability. Thereby, it is great meaningful to explore new stable functional materials with highly efficient sorption and selectivity [5–7].
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are the crystalline regular reticulated porous products by assembly of bridging organic linkers and metal salts or metal clusters as the secondary building units (SBUs). Their varied structures and topological types have attracted scientists to spend a great deal of attention in this field [8–10]. It is well known that the suitable choices of ligands and central metal ions have the crucial roles in the construction of MOFs. The alkaline earth metals, such as Sr-89, Sr-90 and Ba-140, are produced in the uranium fission products [11]; the effective exploration of MOF materials synthesized by alkaline earth metals can thus reduce the environmental pollution. In particular, $\mathbf{Ba(II)}$ ion has the almost largest ionic radius, which may be easily coordinated with much more O atoms to form different stable polynuclear metal clusters/chains as SBUs according to HSAB principle [12–15]. As a result, it is valuable to build new glamorous MOFs based on $\mathbf{Ba(II)}$ ions with various ligands to explore new functional porous materials and enrich coordination chemistry.
Herein, two comparable MOFs $\mathrm{\Delta[Ba(L1)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ (MOF 1) and $[\mathrm{Ba}(\mathrm{L2})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]_{n}{\cdot}0.5n\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}0.5n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (MOF 2) were yielded by successful assembly of oxygen-friendly $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ba}}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{II}})$ ions and two analogous ligands, i.e., the 2-(imidazol-1-yl)terephthalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}1)$ and 2-(1H-1,2,4-triazol1-yl)terephthalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}2)$ . $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray single-crystal diffractions indicate that MOF 1 is a new three-dimensional (3D) stacking dense network formed by the onedimensional (1D) rod-shaped chains and L1 linkers, while MOF 2 is a 3D nanotube porous structure via the 1D loop chains as the SBUs by replacing the imidazole group with triazole group in the ligand. Hence, MOF 2 holds a higher density of active sites and Lewis acid sites in the porous surface, resulting that MOF 2 exhibits a great amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation than that of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ .
