## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Structural description
Structural analysis shows that MOF 1 is in triclinic P-1 space group, and its basic unit includes a $\mathbf{Ba(II)}$ , a L1 linker, two coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and a free $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The $\mathbf{Ba(II)}$ ion is a distorted eight-coordinated configuration with seven O atoms from four carboxylates and a $\mathbf{N}$ atom of ligand (Fig. 1a). MOF 2 crystallizes in the triclinic R-3 space group, and there is a $\mathbf{Ba(II)}$ ion, a L2 ligand, two coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , a half free DMF and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in the building basic unit (Fig. 1b).
It is found that two carboxylates of L1 ligand take two types of coordination modes, bridging tridentate $(\eta^{2}\mu_{2}\chi^{3})$ and bidentate mode $(\eta^{2}\mu_{2}\chi^{2})$ , to link Ba(II) ions in MOF 1 (Fig. S3a), emerging an infinite 1D metal-shaped chain (Fig. S4a). Then, these 1D chains are further elongated by L1 linkers to engender a stacking dense 3D network (Fig. 2a). In MOF 2, each $\mathbf{Ba(II)}$ ions is ten-coordinated geometry with eight $_{0}$ atoms from carboxylates of bridging tridentate $(\eta^{2}\mu_{2}\chi^{3})$ , bridging tetradentate $(\eta^{2}\mu_{4}\chi^{4})$ and bidentate $(\eta^{2}\mu_{2}\chi^{2})$ modes to form the 1D loop chains as SBUs (Figs. S3, S6), and each of the six SBUs thus gives rise to a cylindrical porous tunnel. These tunnels are interconnected by ligands to create an overall 3D net (Fig. 2b). Interestingly, there are abundant active sites and Lewis acid sites on the surface of 1D tunnels, and the free solvents are located in the center of the channels. The potential volume of MOF 2a (the desolvated MOF 2) is determined to be $\sim34.6\%$ after removing the solvents. Topologically, the main structure of MOF 1 may be predigested as a uninodal 5-connected bnn $(4^{6}{\cdot}6^{4})$ network, and similarly, MOF 2 may be simplified as a binodal 5-connected ttd $(4^{5}{\cdot}6^{5})\ (4^{8}{\cdot}6^{2})$ net (Fig. 3).
## 3.2 TGA and PXRD
The thermo-gravimetric analysis (TGA) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) curves of MOFs 1 and 2 were tested (Fig. S5a, S5b), and MOF 2 displays that the first loss of $\sim9.5\%$ (cal.: $9.6\%$ ) below $\sim90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is ascribed to the release of all $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. The main skeleton is
